BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 663.32 Tm (Containment is where the numbers get specific. Balanced Building Biology specs 6 mil fire-retardant poly, zip walls) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 648.10 Tm (and critical barriers over vents and doors, warns never to demolish or place air movers before the room is sealed, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 632.87 Tm (names the crucial metric: hold -5 pascals of negative pressure, tracked with a manometer, using true HEPA at 99.97%) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 617.65 Tm (filtration at 0.3 microns, while shop vacs and M-class extractors are "not allowed" [YdUmzaa5wO0 @ 00:02:47].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 602.42 Tm (Andrew Melrose runs the same kit on mold jobs: 6 mil plastic, negative air, high-CFM HEPA scrubbers, a dehumidifier) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 587.20 Tm (to stop humidity spikes, HEPA vacuums, P100 respirators and decon chambers, and names random chemicals, no) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 571.97 Tm (containment and humidity spikes as the signature of bad work [iQ4PIKW3sEc @ 00:03:54]. Time's Dark Captains adds) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 556.75 Tm (the Texas poly spec of 4 to 6 mil for walls and ceilings, 10 mil for floors, fire-retardant with flame spread of 25 or less,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 541.52 Tm (and reads the standard as not allowing scrubber mode without negative pressure [y00Yi4bnLx4 @ 00:46:31]. Floyd) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 526.30 Tm (puts the consumables on the P&L: AFD pre-filters run $8 to $12, secondaries $15 to $20, and the HEPA about $153,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 511.07 Tm (with carbon filters for odor, and Floyd warns never to draw negative air from a crawlspace because of furnace) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 495.85 Tm (backdraft [cPAFOZbMKQE @ 00:29:36].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 480.62 Tm (Floyd supplies the water-side sizing kit. Drying classes run Class 1 under 5% of surfaces wet, Class 2 at 5 to 40%,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 465.40 Tm (Class 3 over 40%, and Class 4 bound water. Refrigerant dehumidification is cubic feet divided by the class factor to get) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 450.17 Tm (AHAM pints; desiccant is cubic feet times air changes per hour divided by 60 to get CFM. The 2015 air-mover rule is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 434.95 Tm (one per room, plus one per 50 to 70 square feet of floor, plus one per 100 to 150 square feet of wall and ceiling above 2) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 419.72 Tm (ft, plus one per offset over 18 inches, so a 750 square foot loss lands at 12 to 16 units, and water under 2 ft up the wall) Tj ET
